{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984 + spec/interaction-events.md:79: while the run is held open EXPLICITLY, a handler that takes longer than its own interval `degrades to running back to back` (spec/interaction-events.md:189-196) and is bounded by the ordinary instruction budget, which raises `ol-limit` exactly as it does for any other non-terminating program. This is the counterpart of `every-queued-occurrence-discarded-when-run-closes`: there the main line ends and the queue is discarded, here the main line never ends because the learner wrote `forever`, so every queued occurrence is drained at the next checkpoint and the handler never catches up. Both fixtures are needed, and neither alone is sufficient: the discard rule on its own could be satisfied by never draining at all -- the exact defect this issue's first implementation shipped -- and this fixture is what forbids that, because a runtime that drops missed occurrences never accumulates the firings that exhaust the budget. A small `instructionBudget` keeps the trace readable while pinning the same property a default budget would.",
